  ],
  "text": "The pyramids were not built by slaves. We found the workers' village. The slave story comes from writers thousands of years later, not from anyone who was there. At Giza there are bakeries, breweries, and cattle bone for thousands of meals a day. There are worker tombs, built right beside the thing they were building. Nobody buries a slave in sight of the king's monument. And the skeletons show healed fractures. Set properly, and left to mend. Somebody was treating injured workers and feeding them while they could not work. They left graffiti too. Work gangs with names, scrawled where nobody was meant to look. The hard problem was never the stone. It was feeding and organising thousands of people for decades. How the blocks got up there is still genuinely argued about. Who carried them is not. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
